---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="Kikimi: learn a language from videos you own">
  <h1 style="margin-top: 40px">Learn a language from videos you already have.</h1>
  <p>
    Kikimi listens to your videos on your Mac, finds the words you will meet, and helps you learn them with spaced
    repetition, using real sentences from the videos. Spanish, French, German and Japanese, with more to come.
  </p>
  <a class="button" href="https://github.com/surreptus/kikimi/releases/latest">Download for Mac</a>
  <p class="small">
    Free. Needs macOS 15 or later (translations need macOS 26); Apple silicon recommended. It is not notarized yet, so macOS
    asks you to confirm opening it: <a href="https://github.com/surreptus/kikimi#install">here is how</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Add a video.</strong> Drop in a file you own. Nothing is downloaded from video sites.</li>
    <li><strong>It transcribes on your Mac.</strong> A speech model you download once does the work; nothing is uploaded.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose your words.</strong> See what you already know, start learning the rest, and look words up as you watch.</li>
    <li><strong>Practise.</strong> Review words at the right time, each with a sentence from your own videos.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Made for comprehensible input</h2>
  <p>
    Each video shows how much of it you already understand, so you can pick material that is mostly familiar with a
    little new. One button starts learning the commonest words that would make a video comfortable.
  </p>

  <h2>Finding videos to learn from</h2>
  <p>
    Kikimi never finds or downloads videos; it works on files you give it. If you have none, some places publish video
    you may download and study, each under its own licence:
    the <a href="https://archive.org/details/movies">Internet Archive's Moving Image Archive</a> (some items come with subtitle
    files, which Kikimi can use instead of transcribing), <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Videos">Wikimedia
    Commons</a>, and <a href="https://www.ted.com/about/our-organization/our-policies-terms/ted-talks-usage-policy">TED Talks</a>
    for personal use. "Creative Commons" is not one licence: check what each video and its subtitle file allow. This is
    not legal advice.
  </p>

  <h2>Private by design</h2>
  <p>
    No accounts, no ads, no analytics. Your videos, transcripts and progress stay on your Mac. The only time Kikimi
    goes online is to download a speech model when you ask. Read the <a href={`${base}/privacy/`}>privacy policy</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
